'''Xenopeltis''' (zen-ọ̄-pel′tis), ''n.'' [NL. (Reinwardt, 1827), < Gr. ξένος, strange, + πέλτη, a shield.] The typical genus of ''Xenopeltidæ'', having the lower jaw produced, the teeth very fine, and no anal spurs. {{smaller|''X. unicolor'', formerly ''Tortrix xenopeltis'', is a singular snake of nocturnal and carnivorous habits, found in Malaysia and some other regions.}}

'''Xenophanean''' (zē-nof-a-nē′an), ''a.'' [< ''Xenophanes'' (see def.).] Pertaining to the doctrines of Xenophanes of Colophon, the founder of the Eleatic school of philosophy. {{smaller|He seems to have been the first of the Greeks to propound a monotheistic doctrine, probably of a pantheistic character; but he did not go to the length of denying the reality of the manifold, as Parmenides and his followers did.}}

